answer:
Verified — a = {1,2,3}, b = {2,3,4}:

a | b   → {1, 2, 3, 4}      a.union(b)              → {1, 2, 3, 4}
a & b   → {2, 3}            a.intersection(b)       → {2, 3}
a - b   → {1}               a.difference(b)         → {1}
a ^ b   → {1, 4}            a.symmetric_difference(b) → {1, 4}

Bada fayda — method kisi bhi ITERABLE ko leta hai, operator sirf set:

a.union([9])           # {1, 2, 3, 9}
a.intersection([2])    # {2}

Jabki `a | [9]` par TypeError aata hai (`unsupported operand type`).

Aur ek constraint: `issubset`/`issuperset` ke ARGUMENT bhi hashable elements wale iterable hone chahiye:

{1, 2}.issubset([[1], [2]])
# TypeError: unhashable type: 'list'

Extra notes:
· In-place versions: `a |= b`, `a &= b`, `a -= b`, `a ^= b` aur `a.intersection_update(b)` type methods — poore set ko mutate karte hain (return None) aur naye objects nahi banate.
· `|=` set ko mutate karta hai, jabki `a = a | b` rebind karta hai — aliasing wale code me farq matter karta hai.
· operators chained ho sakte hain (`a | b | c`) aur numeric bitwise operators ke saath same syntax share karte hain (sets plain integer values par bitwise ka kaam karte hain, par semantics set algebra hai).

Interview line: "Operators need both sides to be sets; methods accept any iterable — that's why I use `.union(list)` when the other side is a list."

s = {1, 1, 2, 3}
print(s, len(s), 2 in s)
# {1, 2, 3} 3 True

print(set([1, 1, 2, 3]), set('banana'), set((1, 2, 2)))
# {1, 2, 3} {'a', 'b', 'n'} {1, 2}

try:
    s[0]
except TypeError as e:
    print('TypeError:', e)
# TypeError: 'set' object is not subscriptable

print(sorted(s), list(s))
# [1, 2, 3] [1, 2, 3]      ← deterministic output chahiye to sorted()

s = {1, 2}
s.add(3)
print(s)
# {1, 2, 3}
s.update([4, 5])
print(s)
# {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}
s.remove(5)
